<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Mindelheim</h1><p class="st-meta">1705 CE – 1714 CE · capital Mindelheim</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Swabian lordship briefly made a principality of the Empire for the Duke of Marlborough as a reward for Blenheim.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindelheim">Mindelheim</a>, a Swabian lordship belonging to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/electorate-of-bavaria">Electorate of Bavaria</a>, was confiscated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-i-holy-roman-emperor">Emperor Joseph I</a> after the Bavarian elector's defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-blenheim">Blenheim</a> in 1704. In 1705 it was granted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-churchill-1st-duke-of-marlborough">John Churchill</a>, Duke of Marlborough, as a principality, making him a prince of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-churchill-1st-duke-of-marlborough">Marlborough</a> never lived there and visited only briefly; the lordship was administered by agents who collected its revenues from the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindelheim">Mindelheim</a> and surrounding villages. Its importance was symbolic, giving the English general rank among the German princes. The population remained Catholic under a Protestant absentee prince.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>At the peace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-rastatt">Rastatt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> in 1714 the Elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> was restored to his lands, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindelheim">Mindelheim</a> returned to Bavaria. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-churchill-1st-duke-of-marlborough">Marlborough</a> kept the title of Prince of Mindelheim, and the episode is remembered in the town's history.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-mindelheim.jpg" alt="Principality of Mindelheim" width="320" height="262"><figcaption>Image: Scanned, cleaned, etc., and uploaded here, by Maproom · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mindelheim_map_Coxe.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1705 CE – 1714 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mindelheim</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>John Churchill, Duke of Marlborough</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lordship of Mindelheim</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1710&amp;focus=principality-of-mindelheim" aria-label="Principality of Mindelheim on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4924 496 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Mindelheim lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5244 752L5229 694M5244 752L5269 749"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5269 749h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5244 752h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5244 752h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Mindelheim. <a href="/atlas?year=1710&amp;focus=principality-of-mindelheim">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
